---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';
import { OUTCOMES, FACTS, KEY_VARIABLE } from '../data/catalog.mjs';
import { cell } from '../data/examples.mjs';

const lines = cell('decide', 'shell').runs.find((r) => r.name === 'decide-lines');
---
<Base title="Trust" description="Outcomes, thresholds, the cache, what leaves the machine, and how to check a question against your own labeled records.">
  <h1>Test it before you trust it</h1>
  <p class="lede">ThinkThen gives every answer a fixed form and the model’s probability. You set the bar, and you count the misses on your own records.</p>

  <h2>Four outcomes, four colors</h2>
  <p>Learn the exit codes by looking. The same four colors run through this site, the terminal, and the diagrams.</p>
  <div class="outcomes">
    {OUTCOMES.map((o) => (
      <div class={`outcome ${o.key}`}>
        <b>{o.label}</b>
        <span>exit {o.code} · prints <code>{o.prints}</code></span>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>The threshold is yours</h2>
  <p>The model reports a probability. Your threshold turns it into an answer, and nothing else does. One number is a cut. Two numbers are a band, and the middle is not sure.</p>
  <p>Three messages under one band:</p>
  <Terminal run={lines} caption="Recorded from the real command. The refund request is yes and the thank-you is no. The send-back line could mean an exchange or money back, so its 0.54 lands in the band as null." />
  <p>You choose the threshold. The model never does.</p>
  <p><code>jev-latest</code> follows the vendor’s newest model. Pin a run by naming a version, and check your labeled cases again when you change it.</p>

  <h2>Not sure is an answer</h2>
  <p>A band is how a pipeline hands a person the calls it should not make alone. <code>null</code> and exit 3 mean the probability landed in the middle you set.</p>
  <p>A question the backend could not answer is different. It is marked failed and counted, and it never turns into <code>null</code>. <code>annotate</code> exits 6 when a run finishes with failed questions.</p>

  <h2>The words for the numbers</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Word</th><th>What it is</th></tr>
    <tr><td>probability</td><td>A number the model reported, passed through unchanged. For yes or no, the chance the answer is yes. For pick one and place on a scale, the share given to each option or level.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>probabilities</td><td>The model's full list, one number per option or level, adding to 1. It shows under <code>--details</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>threshold</td><td>Your bar on a probability. An input.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>band</td><td>A low bar and a high bar. The middle goes to a person.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>position</td><td>The number <code>score</code> prints: where the evidence lands along your levels. It is the average of the level probabilities, and we compute it.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>strength</td><td>The number on a recognized name. We compute it, and it claims nothing about chance.</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>We do not print a confidence of our own. The one field named confidence is the vendor's number for how lopsided the probabilities are, it shows only under <code>--details</code>, and it failed as a gate in our tests.</p>
  <p>The test was experiment 220, on the words <code>recognize</code> reads. As a gate between name words and ordinary words, <code>confidence</code> scored 0.22 on a scale where 0.5 is chance and 1 is perfect. The probability of the picked option scored 0.99. The record is <code>sdlc/issues/2026-09-21-one-rule-for-every-number-the-tool-prints.md</code>.</p>

  <h2>What leaves the machine</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The question and your text go to the backend in one request. Nothing else does. With <code>--field</code>, only the part you point at goes.</li>
    <li>The tool sends your text as it is. It splits nothing and drops nothing.</li>
    <li>The key comes from <code>{KEY_VARIABLE}</code> in the environment. No flag and no file carries one.</li>
    <li><code>--dry-run</code> prints the plan and sends nothing.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Saved answers</h2>
  <p>The tool saves what it asked and what came back. Ask the same question of the same evidence again and it reads the saved answer instead of sending a request, at no further cost. Saving is on by default, and <code>--no-cache</code> turns it off for one run. On SQLite, <code>thinkthen_warm</code> judges a table in one pass and every query after that reads the saved answers.</p>

  <h2>How big the evidence can be</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Fact</th><th>Where it was measured</th></tr>
    {FACTS.size.rows.map(([fact, where]) => <tr><td>{fact}</td><td>{where}</td></tr>)}
  </table>

  <h2>What it costs</h2>
  <p>Requests are the bill. A short record pays almost only for the fixed part. Many questions about one piece of evidence pay the fixed part once. <code>annotate</code> does that. Many records pay it once each.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Fact</th><th>Where it was measured</th></tr>
    {FACTS.cost.rows.map(([fact, where]) => <tr><td>{fact}</td><td>{where}</td></tr>)}
  </table>

  <h2>Check a question against your own labeled records</h2>
  <p>Do not take a question on trust. Take the records you have already labeled, run the question over them with <code>--details</code>, and compare the answer to your label. Then move the threshold and look again.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Put your labeled records in a JSONL file, one object each, with your own label on a field.</li>
    <li>Run <code>thinkthen decide --jsonl --details --field /text 'your question' &lt; labeled.jsonl</code>.</li>
    <li>Compare <code>.value</code> to your label with <code>jq</code>, and count the misses.</li>
    <li>Try a band. The pairs that land in the middle are the ones a person should read.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Only a number from your own records tells you how it does on your work.</p>

  <h2>What it will not do</h2>
  <p>The <a href="/refusals/">refusals page</a> lists every line the tool will not cross.</p>

  <p class="pagelinks"><a href="/functions/decide/">Run decide</a> · <a href="/functions/question-file/">Save a question file</a> · <a href="/refusals/">See what it will not do</a> · <a href="/install/">Install it</a></p>
</Base>
